["<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"Female Testosterone Falls 1% a Year, Brain Metabolism 20% in Perimenopause Women should establish a hormone baseline in their twenties, when testosterone is already falling about 1% a year; oral contraceptives lower ovarian cancer risk while possibly shrinking the clitoris by up to 20%; brain glucose metabolism drops 20% on average in perimenopause, and hot flashes are a disease signal, not a minor nuisance. Huberman Lab Huberman Lab Essentials: How to Optimize Female Hormone Health for Vitality &amp; Longevity | Dr. Sara Gottfried Female Hormones Testosterone Perimenopause Oral Contraceptives Brain Metabolism Continuous Glucose Monitoring SHBG / sex hormone-binding globulin CGM / continuous glucose monitor PCOS / polycystic ovary syndrome FDG PET / fluorodeoxyglucose positron emission tomography HPA axis / hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis CAC / coronary artery calcium score\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-13-huberman-女性激素真相-睾酮流失与围绝经期脑代谢/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/a1ac1c82ed5e5947-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"H\"><span class=\"dur\">38:19</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Huberman Lab</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0813</span></div>\n<h2>Female Testosterone Falls 1% a Year, Brain Metabolism 20% in Perimenopause</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Women should establish a hormone baseline in their twenties, when testosterone is already falling about 1% a year; oral contraceptives lower ovarian cancer risk while possibly shrinking the clitoris by up to 20%; brain glucose metabolism drops 20% on average in perimenopause, and hot flashes are a disease signal, not a minor nuisance.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Female Hormones</span><span class=\"tag\">Testosterone</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"The trucking rate spike is enforcement cutting supply, not demand returning English language proficiency enforcement, a crackdown on non-domiciled CDLs and a Supreme Court ruling on broker liability pulled capacity out at the same time. What ended a three-year trough was not freight volume coming back but a structural rise in the barrier to entry — so the next demand surge will hit a market that can no longer add supply the way it did during COVID. Odd Lots Odd Lots Trucking Is Booming Again, And Drivers Aren&#x27;t Happy About It Trucking Regulation Supply contraction Cyclical stocks Physical infrastructure Labor non-domiciled CDL ELD (electronic logging device) freight broker FMCSA safety rating spot vs contract rate owner operator\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-13-oddlots-这轮卡车运价上涨来自供给被砍-不是需求回来了/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/dbb9160e6d589beb-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"O\"><span class=\"dur\">46:21</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Odd Lots</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0813</span></div>\n<h2>The trucking rate spike is enforcement cutting supply, not demand returning</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">English language proficiency enforcement, a crackdown on non-domiciled CDLs and a Supreme Court ruling on broker liability pulled capacity out at the same time. What ended a three-year trough was not freight volume coming back but a structural rise in the barrier to entry — so the next demand surge will hit a market that can no longer add supply the way it did during COVID.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Trucking</span><span class=\"tag\">Regulation</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"After Tupperware fired its queen, the company buried her book Brownie Wise turned Tupperware into an American household brand by selling it through housewives&#x27; living-room parties, then was fired for outshining the inventor, had the record of her life destroyed, and got no official recognition until a park was named for her in 2016. Throughline Throughline The blatant betrayal that erased the Tupperware queen from history Sales Revolution Women at Work Brand History American Business Organizational Power subversive domesticity burp Wonder Bowl Jubilee\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-13-throughline-特百惠女王遭公然背叛-被美国历史抹去/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/6899c998b8f2c10a-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">49:11</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Throughline</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0813</span></div>\n<h2>After Tupperware fired its queen, the company buried her book</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Brownie Wise turned Tupperware into an American household brand by selling it through housewives&#x27; living-room parties, then was fired for outshining the inventor, had the record of her life destroyed, and got no official recognition until a park was named for her in 2016.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>4</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Sales Revolution</span><span class=\"tag\">Women at Work</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"LLMs industrialize the con, and single sign-on becomes the master switch When faking a job interview, monitoring an inbox and synthesizing a voice all cost close to nothing, anyone&#x27;s SSO account can be taken over silently; who the bank makes absorb the loss matters more than teaching users to spot phishing. Complex Systems Complex Systems LLMs and the economics of Evil, Inc., with Manish Goregaokar LLMs Anti-fraud Money laundering Cybersecurity Social engineering Supply chain attacks BEC (business email compromise) money mule AML (anti-money laundering) Regulation E desk name supply chain attack\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-13-complexsys-大模型把骗局工业化-单点登录成了总开关/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/03fbe8332bb0a87a-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"C\"><span class=\"dur\">1:02:34</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Complex Systems</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0813</span></div>\n<h2>LLMs industrialize the con, and single sign-on becomes the master switch</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">When faking a job interview, monitoring an inbox and synthesizing a voice all cost close to nothing, anyone&#x27;s SSO account can be taken over silently; who the bank makes absorb the loss matters more than teaching users to spot phishing.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">LLMs</span><span class=\"tag\">Anti-fraud</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"Romans knew the founding myth was false and used it as a diplomatic tool Rome&#x27;s origin myths were not history carved in stone but an elastic narrative Romans used to negotiate, to absorb other peoples, and to digest civil war; the archaeology even contradicts the 753 BC founding date outright. The Ancients The Ancients Romulus &amp; Remus Roman history Political mythology Archaeology Aeneas Romulus Oral tradition Rex Pomerium Casa Romuli Lupa\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-13-ancients-罗马人清楚建城神话是虚构的-但照样当外交工具/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/a9395e3e4c90e300-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">59:55</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">The Ancients</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0813</span></div>\n<h2>Romans knew the founding myth was false and used it as a diplomatic tool</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Rome&#x27;s origin myths were not history carved in stone but an elastic narrative Romans used to negotiate, to absorb other peoples, and to digest civil war; the archaeology even contradicts the 753 BC founding date outright.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Roman history</span><span class=\"tag\">Political mythology</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"Elizabeth wanted Mary assassinated, but was forced to sign the death warrant Elizabeth I did not want Mary executed in public; she hoped someone would kill her privately under the Bond of Association. The Privy Council insisted on legal process, and in the end, even after the Queen had signed, they defied her wishes and rushed the warrant through the night to Fotheringhay. The Rest Is History The Rest Is History 696. Elizabeth I vs The Catholics: Killing the Queen of Scots (Part 6) Elizabeth I Mary Stuart British history Intelligence war Cryptography Death penalty politics Bond of Association Act for the Queen&#x27;s Safety presence chamber Star Chamber cipher\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-12-restishistory-玛丽之死背后的真博弈-伊丽莎白想要暗杀-不想签字/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/4d082c6220b28920-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:09:49</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">The Rest Is History</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0812</span></div>\n<h2>Elizabeth wanted Mary assassinated, but was forced to sign the death warrant</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Elizabeth I did not want Mary executed in public; she hoped someone would kill her privately under the Bond of Association. The Privy Council insisted on legal process, and in the end, even after the Queen had signed, they defied her wishes and rushed the warrant through the night to Fotheringhay.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Elizabeth I</span><span class=\"tag\">Mary Stuart</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"cn\" data-hay=\"NVIDIA&#x27;s VP of Research: The Endgame for Models Is Ecosystem, Not Beating Rivals Ming-Yu Liu: model capabilities will eventually converge, and NVIDIA&#x27;s open-source world model is not competition but groundwork; physical AI&#x27;s GPT moment has not arrived, but the conditions are converging. 张小珺·商业访谈录 张小珺·商业访谈录 150. 对英伟达研究副总裁刘洺堉的4小时访谈：功夫、Cosmos 3、开源世界模型、黄仁勋、你不需要击败所有对手 World models Cosmos NVIDIA Jensen Huang Physical AI Robotics Physical AI World Foundation Model Multimodal Transformer Dual-tower\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-12-zhangxiaojun-英伟达研究副总裁-模型竞争终局是生态-不是击败对手/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/920e2373c894c74b-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"张\"><span class=\"dur\">3:36:12</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"cn\"><span class=\"src\" lang=\"zh\">张小珺·商业访谈录</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0812</span></div>\n<h2>NVIDIA&#x27;s VP of Research: The Endgame for Models Is Ecosystem, Not Beating Rivals</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Ming-Yu Liu: model capabilities will eventually converge, and NVIDIA&#x27;s open-source world model is not competition but groundwork; physical AI&#x27;s GPT moment has not arrived, but the conditions are converging.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">World models</span><span class=\"tag\">Cosmos</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"Bedtime procrastination isn&#x27;t laziness — it&#x27;s the self-time your day took away For most people bedtime procrastination runs only about half an hour; the real problem is a daytime with no autonomy. Blue-light glasses do less than advertised, while indoor lighting matters more. And self-compassion improves sleep more than forcing yourself into bed early. Ologies with Alie Ward Ologies Noctohabitology (SLEEP PROCRASTINATION) with Vanessa “Nessy” Hill Sleep science Behavioral psychology Bedtime procrastination ADHD Self-compassion Sleep procrastination Circadian rhythm Cortisol\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-12-ologies-睡前拖延不是懒-是白天没了自我时间/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/6714d8dbd3a57d1f-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"O\"><span class=\"dur\">1:37:32</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Ologies with Alie Ward</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0812</span></div>\n<h2>Bedtime procrastination isn&#x27;t laziness — it&#x27;s the self-time your day took away</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">For most people bedtime procrastination runs only about half an hour; the real problem is a daytime with no autonomy. Blue-light glasses do less than advertised, while indoor lighting matters more. And self-compassion improves sleep more than forcing yourself into bed early.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>2</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Sleep science</span><span class=\"tag\">Behavioral psychology</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"You don&#x27;t have to read AI&#x27;s code line by line, but trust has to be rebuilt AI in 2025 is where the cloud was in 2010. The real question is not whether to read the code AI writes, but when not to read it, and how to rebuild trust with tests, evals, and an honest accounting of the on-call cost. The Pragmatic Engineer The Pragmatic Engineer Stop being skeptical about AI for development with Charity Majors AI coding code review observability platform engineering engineering leadership AI fatigue harness conformance testing slop SEV zero doom trolling\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-12-pragmatic-ai-写代码不必逐行读-但信任必须重建/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/5f2a55d9c9f0838c-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:25:32</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The Pragmatic Engineer</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0812</span></div>\n<h2>You don&#x27;t have to read AI&#x27;s code line by line, but trust has to be rebuilt</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">AI in 2025 is where the cloud was in 2010. The real question is not whether to read the code AI writes, but when not to read it, and how to rebuild trust with tests, evals, and an honest accounting of the on-call cost.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI coding</span><span class=\"tag\">code review</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"A Diabetes Drug That Switches Itself On With Blood Sugar: Live Bacteria Secreting GLP1 on Demand Scientists have engineered E. coli to secrete GLP1 automatically when blood sugar runs high, improving glucose control in mice and in macaques; human trials are still a long way off, but the work opens up the prospect of a living drug that doses on demand. Nature Podcast Nature Podcast The probiotic bacteria engineered to treat diabetes Probiotics Diabetes GLP-1 Genetic engineering Living medicines Solar eclipse GLP-1 (glucagon-like peptide-1) Molecular brake Proba-3\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-12-naturepod-活菌当药-让糖尿病治疗随血糖自动开关/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/9b247947db171866-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"N\"><span class=\"dur\">19:37</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Nature Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0812</span></div>\n<h2>A Diabetes Drug That Switches Itself On With Blood Sugar: Live Bacteria Secreting GLP1 on Demand</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Scientists have engineered E. coli to secrete GLP1 automatically when blood sugar runs high, improving glucose control in mice and in macaques; human trials are still a long way off, but the work opens up the prospect of a living drug that doses on demand.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Probiotics</span><span class=\"tag\">Diabetes</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"AI writing has stalled: long-form nonfiction is the real test of model ability Models are improving only slowly at long-form nonfiction, which exposes a defect in how they organize knowledge — a more worrying signal than their progress in code and math, because it bears on whether AI can solve open scientific problems. Interconnects Interconnects I wrote an AI textbook — how long until AI can do it better? AI writing nonfiction technical writing model capability RLHF scientific progress RLVR RLHF inference-time scaling\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-12-interconnects-ai写作停滞-长文非虚构是模型能力的试金石/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/ba2952184132ad60-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"I\"><span class=\"dur\">12:32</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Interconnects</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0812</span></div>\n<h2>AI writing has stalled: long-form nonfiction is the real test of model ability</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Models are improving only slowly at long-form nonfiction, which exposes a defect in how they organize knowledge — a more worrying signal than their progress in code and math, because it bears on whether AI can solve open scientific problems.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>9</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI writing</span><span class=\"tag\">nonfiction</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"The father of linoleum never trademarked it, and even the name became public property Frederick Walton invented linoleum but lost the name because he never registered it as a trademark; the process that actually set the industry standard came from a man on his payroll, C.F. Leak. Stuff You Missed in History Class Stuff You Missed in History Class Frederick Walton and the Invention of Linoleum Invention History Linoleum Patents 19th-Century Britain Materials Industry Family Business linoleum frizzing Camptulicon Lincrusta-Walton\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-12-stuffmissed-油毡之父没注册商标-连名字都成了公共财产/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/88586eb98e196b1a-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">41:32</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Stuff You Missed in History Class</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0812</span></div>\n<h2>The father of linoleum never trademarked it, and even the name became public property</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Frederick Walton invented linoleum but lost the name because he never registered it as a trademark; the process that actually set the industry standard came from a man on his payroll, C.F. Leak.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Invention History</span><span class=\"tag\">Linoleum</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Liberalism Died of the Educated Elite, Not Automation Acemoglu dismantles three myths: liberalism is dying of the educated elite rather than automation; AI productivity will not break out for a decade; regulating algorithms is not a restriction on speech, and pro-worker AI is the direction. Conversations with Tyler Conversations with Tyler Daron Acemoglu on Liberalism, Automation, and the Educated Elite Automation Labor share AI productivity Free speech Liberalism Educated elite non-domination task-based framework pro worker AI agentic AI\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-12-tyler-精英化杀死自由主义-自动化并非元凶/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/16eb2a1660e908ef-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"C\"><span class=\"dur\">55:38</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">Conversations with Tyler</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0812</span></div>\n<h2>Liberalism Died of the Educated Elite, Not Automation</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Acemoglu dismantles three myths: liberalism is dying of the educated elite rather than automation; AI productivity will not break out for a decade; regulating algorithms is not a restriction on speech, and pro-worker AI is the direction.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Automation</span><span class=\"tag\">Labor share</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Behind a 0.33Å Error: Drug Design Is Moving From Waterfall to Agile Loop Chai designed antibodies for 50 targets and got hits on about half of them, with cryo-EM validation showing a 0.33Å error. The real moat is not the model — it is data, compute, and a validation pipeline pharma companies will actually pay for. Latent Space Latent Space 🔬The BioAI Phase Shift - Matthew McPartlon &amp; Neil Patil, Chai Discovery Biopharma Structure prediction Antibody design Compute AI4S binder epitope MSA (multiple sequence alignment) cryo-EM durable execution bitter lesson\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-11-latentspace-0-33å-误差背后的信号-药物发现正转向敏捷闭环/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/12960e9021912df5-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"L\"><span class=\"dur\">1:35:19</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Latent Space</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0811</span></div>\n<h2>Behind a 0.33Å Error: Drug Design Is Moving From Waterfall to Agile Loop</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Chai designed antibodies for 50 targets and got hits on about half of them, with cryo-EM validation showing a 0.33Å error. The real moat is not the model — it is data, compute, and a validation pipeline pharma companies will actually pay for.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Biopharma</span><span class=\"tag\">Structure prediction</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"A 4x-Levered Bet on the AI Narrative: How a Genius Fund Died of Volatility Drag Aschenbrenner&#x27;s failure was not a failure of intelligence. It was what happens when you put 3-4x leverage behind a single high-volatility AI theme: volatility drag and asymmetric payoffs made losing control a matter of arithmetic. Markets do not reward concentrated, narrative-driven bets. Patrick Boyle Patrick Boyle Does Leopold Have Situational Awareness? AI narrative Leverage Volatility drag Hedge funds Silicon Valley culture Risk management volatility drag margin call expected return long/short equity lockup agreement full Kelly\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-11-patrickboyle-天才叙事败给波动率拖累-纸上英雄到破产只需一场婚礼/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/2eb71149af422d0e-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"P\"><span class=\"dur\">39:37</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Patrick Boyle</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0811</span></div>\n<h2>A 4x-Levered Bet on the AI Narrative: How a Genius Fund Died of Volatility Drag</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Aschenbrenner&#x27;s failure was not a failure of intelligence. It was what happens when you put 3-4x leverage behind a single high-volatility AI theme: volatility drag and asymmetric payoffs made losing control a matter of arithmetic. Markets do not reward concentrated, narrative-driven bets.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI narrative</span><span class=\"tag\">Leverage</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Once AI automates AI research, losing control looks like sloppiness, not malice Ryan Greenblatt argues AI R&amp;D is verifiable enough that full automation would compress four to five years of progress into one; but the real loss-of-control path is reward hacking getting papered over again and again, not AI suddenly turning evil. He puts the probability of a takeover by 2040 at 35-40%. Dwarkesh Podcast Dwarkesh Ryan Greenblatt – What happens once AI can automate AI research? recursive self-improvement alignment reward hacking RL environments algorithmic progress model specs recursive self-improvement (RSI) reward hacking grader sandbagging situational awareness sloppocalypse\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-11-dwarkesh-ai-自动化-ai-研发之后-最大的风险不是恶意而是马虎/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/e56b28ccc889689b-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"D\"><span class=\"dur\">2:12:32</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Dwarkesh Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0811</span></div>\n<h2>Once AI automates AI research, losing control looks like sloppiness, not malice</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Ryan Greenblatt argues AI R&amp;D is verifiable enough that full automation would compress four to five years of progress into one; but the real loss-of-control path is reward hacking getting papered over again and again, not AI suddenly turning evil. He puts the probability of a takeover by 2040 at 35-40%.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>10</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">recursive self-improvement</span><span class=\"tag\">alignment</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"The window to slow down has already closed; superintelligence arrives in two to three years Geoffrey Irving, the UK&#x27;s former head of AI safety science, argues that full superintelligence is two to three years away and the window for slowing down has already passed; the hope for alignment lies in scalable oversight, character research and theoretical breakthroughs, not in precisely specifying a utility function. 80,000 Hours Podcast 80,000 Hours #251 – The UK&#x27;s former head AI safety scientist on how to solve alignment before superintelligence arrives | Geoffrey Irving AI alignment Superintelligence Slowing down Scalable oversight AISI AGI scalable oversight ambiguous arguments character training effective field theory data flywheel\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-11-eightythousand-减速应始于现在-超级智能两三年内到来/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/4d12aa87b0cb730e-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"8\"><span class=\"dur\">2:02:16</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">80,000 Hours Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0811</span></div>\n<h2>The window to slow down has already closed; superintelligence arrives in two to three years</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Geoffrey Irving, the UK&#x27;s former head of AI safety science, argues that full superintelligence is two to three years away and the window for slowing down has already passed; the hope for alignment lies in scalable oversight, character research and theoretical breakthroughs, not in precisely specifying a utility function.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI alignment</span><span class=\"tag\">Superintelligence</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"parent\" data-hay=\"Don&#x27;t stand between your kid and the screen — stand beside them The real mechanism of toxic stress is exposure without an adult to buffer it; the more you can be on your kid&#x27;s side, with technology as the shared opponent, the more the relationship itself becomes the strongest protection available in the digital age. Ask Lisa: The Psychology of Raising Tweens &amp; Teens Ask Lisa 284: ENCORE: Screens, Toxic Stress &amp; Staying Connected with Dr. Nadine Burke Harris Screen time Toxic stress ACEs Adolescence AI companions Parent-child relationship ACEs / adverse childhood experiences toxic stress response buffering caregiving upstander\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-11-asklisa-别站在孩子和屏幕之间-要站在孩子这边/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/ace27132dfc1e3aa-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"A\"><span class=\"dur\">47:23</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"parent\"><span class=\"src\">Ask Lisa: The Psychology of Raising Tweens &amp; Teens</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0811</span></div>\n<h2>Don&#x27;t stand between your kid and the screen — stand beside them</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The real mechanism of toxic stress is exposure without an adult to buffer it; the more you can be on your kid&#x27;s side, with technology as the shared opponent, the more the relationship itself becomes the strongest protection available in the digital age.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Screen time</span><span class=\"tag\">Toxic stress</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"Deep-tech companies die of organizational failure, not technical failure How fast a startup can iterate determines how long it can keep winning — and iteration speed comes from &quot;infrastructure&quot; like procurement, hiring and performance review. Moving approvals up to the budget layer and replacing reviews with eigenreviews is worth more investment than agonizing over any specific technology. Y Combinator Y Combinator Max Hodak: How Startups Build Speed Organizational efficiency Hiring mechanics Performance review Startup fundraising Deep tech Brain-computer interfaces eigenreviews AI-resistant homework vibe coding tropicalization\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-10-ycsp-深科技公司败给组织系统-而不是技术失败/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/d5e8b9a2ac9beab2-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"Y\"><span class=\"dur\">57:02</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Y Combinator</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0810</span></div>\n<h2>Deep-tech companies die of organizational failure, not technical failure</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">How fast a startup can iterate determines how long it can keep winning — and iteration speed comes from &quot;infrastructure&quot; like procurement, hiring and performance review. Moving approvals up to the budget layer and replacing reviews with eigenreviews is worth more investment than agonizing over any specific technology.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Organizational efficiency</span><span class=\"tag\">Hiring mechanics</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"Smashing threshing machines bought 252 death sentences, and the workers still lost Nineteenth-century English farm laborers protested unemployment by destroying threshing machines. What they got was 252 death sentences, 481 or 505 people transported to Australia, and the limited parliamentary reform of 1832 — the laborers&#x27; fortunes did not change, but the political map did. Stuff You Missed in History Class Stuff You Missed in History Class Swing Riots Agricultural Revolution Technological Unemployment British History Parliamentary Reform Machine-Breaking Poor Laws threshing machine flail Corn Laws workhouse select vestries rotten boroughs\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-10-stuffmissed-砸烂打谷机也没能换来工人好日子/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/88586eb98e196b1a-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">36:34</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Stuff You Missed in History Class</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0810</span></div>\n<h2>Smashing threshing machines bought 252 death sentences, and the workers still lost</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Nineteenth-century English farm laborers protested unemployment by destroying threshing machines. What they got was 252 death sentences, 481 or 505 people transported to Australia, and the limited parliamentary reform of 1832 — the laborers&#x27; fortunes did not change, but the political map did.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Agricultural Revolution</span><span class=\"tag\">Technological Unemployment</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Custom Silicon Isn&#x27;t About Saving Money, It&#x27;s About Escaping Nvidia&#x27;s Generational Grip The real motive behind hyperscaler custom silicon is not cost but control: not being held hostage to Nvidia&#x27;s generational cadence, while using sheer scale to squeeze down networking and compute costs. The Broadcom tax is cheaper than the Nvidia tax, but only the largest players can afford to play. TechTechPotato TechTechPotato What do we actually think about Hyperscale Silicon?? // The AI Hardware Podcast S2E9 Hyperscalers Custom silicon AI hardware Nvidia Arm TPU Nitro RISC-V Arm CSS\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-10-techtechpotato-超大规模自研芯片-不只为省钱-更怕被-nvidia-绑架/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/430136289cd55797-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">30:20</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">TechTechPotato</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0810</span></div>\n<h2>Custom Silicon Isn&#x27;t About Saving Money, It&#x27;s About Escaping Nvidia&#x27;s Generational Grip</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The real motive behind hyperscaler custom silicon is not cost but control: not being held hostage to Nvidia&#x27;s generational cadence, while using sheer scale to squeeze down networking and compute costs. The Broadcom tax is cheaper than the Nvidia tax, but only the largest players can afford to play.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Hyperscalers</span><span class=\"tag\">Custom silicon</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"LLMs Are Not Autocomplete — They Are Our Cognitive Cousins LLMs are not fancy autocomplete but ‘cognitive cousins’ that share core principles with human cognition: attention mechanisms recreate production systems, and prediction is the dominant training signal; sample efficiency is three to four orders of magnitude worse, but an architectural change may close the gap. Sean Carroll&#x27;s Mindscape Mindscape 363 | Chandra Sripada on How LLMs and Humans are Cognitive Cousins Cognitive science Cognitive cousins Production systems Dual-process theory Predictive coding LLM mechanisms production system in-weight processing in-context processing contravariance architectural canalization chain of thought\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-10-mindscape-预测是训练信号之母-llm-是认知表亲/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/d69097efcbd03611-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">1:51:36</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">Sean Carroll&#x27;s Mindscape</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0810</span></div>\n<h2>LLMs Are Not Autocomplete — They Are Our Cognitive Cousins</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">LLMs are not fancy autocomplete but ‘cognitive cousins’ that share core principles with human cognition: attention mechanisms recreate production systems, and prediction is the dominant training signal; sample efficiency is three to four orders of magnitude worse, but an architectural change may close the gap.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Cognitive science</span><span class=\"tag\">Cognitive cousins</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Death Is the Last Enemy, Not the Final Word: A Former Senator on Borrowed Time Ben Sasse, a former senator with late-stage pancreatic cancer, gives this interview on borrowed time: his tumor has shrunk by more than 80%, but the disease may not be curable. He calls death ‘the last enemy, but not the final word’, and traces executive overreach back to legislative retreat. EconTalk EconTalk Ben Sasse on Faith, Family, and Facing Death Pancreatic cancer Facing death University governance Executive power Faith Digital revolution Heidelberg Catechism shared governance accelerationism safetyism\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-10-econtalk-把死亡视为最后的敌人而非最终话语/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/f8da698e33560635-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"E\"><span class=\"dur\">1:21:11</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">EconTalk</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0810</span></div>\n<h2>Death Is the Last Enemy, Not the Final Word: A Former Senator on Borrowed Time</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Ben Sasse, a former senator with late-stage pancreatic cancer, gives this interview on borrowed time: his tumor has shrunk by more than 80%, but the disease may not be curable. He calls death ‘the last enemy, but not the final word’, and traces executive overreach back to legislative retreat.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Pancreatic cancer</span><span class=\"tag\">Facing death</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Postliterate: Everyone Is Reading, and No One Reads at Length Anymore The share of Americans who read a book or an article on a given day has fallen 40% in twenty years; the thing to watch is not a literacy crisis but postliteracy — abstract thought, schooling and political communication are exiting alongside long-form reading. The Gray Area with Sean Illing The Gray Area We’re postliterate Postliteracy Attention economy Education Political communication Media theory Walter Ong postliterate secondary orality syllogism epithet\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-10-grayarea-后文字时代-人人都在读-却没人再读长文/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/7c8b1deddea1e240-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">51:41</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">The Gray Area with Sean Illing</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0810</span></div>\n<h2>Postliterate: Everyone Is Reading, and No One Reads at Length Anymore</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The share of Americans who read a book or an article on a given day has fallen 40% in twenty years; the thing to watch is not a literacy crisis but postliteracy — abstract thought, schooling and political communication are exiting alongside long-form reading.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Postliteracy</span><span class=\"tag\">Attention economy</span>\n</div></div></a>"]